@page "/architecture"
<PageTitle>How WattWeather works</PageTitle>
<section class="page-hero">
    <div><span class="kicker">UNDER THE CLOUDS</span><h1>Small app. <em>Solid weather stack.</em></h1><p>WattWeather is a focused .NET weather app built to make live API data feel fast, friendly, and clear.</p></div>
</section>
<section class="page-section">
    <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="feature-card"><span>01 / BLAZOR</span><h3>C# in the browser</h3><p>Blazor WebAssembly powers the search, responsive forecast, sharing, and loading states.</p></article>
        <article class="feature-card"><span>02 / OPEN-METEO</span><h3>Live weather</h3><p>Geocoding finds U.S. cities and ZIP codes; forecast endpoints return current conditions and seven daily outlooks.</p></article>
        <article class="feature-card"><span>03 / ASP.NET</span><h3>Optional secure API</h3><p>The server edition validates coordinates, rate-limits requests, caches responses, and adds security headers.</p></article>
        <article class="feature-card"><span>04 / XUNIT</span><h3>Tested details</h3><p>Tests cover weather-code labels, emoji choices, wind direction, URL construction, input validation, and backend security.</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="method"><strong>Privacy:</strong> no account and no personal tracking. The browser saves only the last location so the forecast is ready when you return.</div>
</section>
